### What is tight coupling vs loose coupling?

Coupling measures how strongly one class depends on another.

### What does tight coupling look like?

```java
public class TradeService {
    private final SmsService smsService = new SmsService();          // hardwired
    private final PostgresTradeRepository tradeRepo = new PostgresTradeRepository(); // hardwired

    public void executeTrade(Trade trade) {
        tradeRepo.save(trade);                                       // locked to PostgreSQL
        smsService.send(trade.getAccountId(), "Trade executed");     // locked to SMS
    }
}
// Cannot switch to EmailService without modifying TradeService.
// Cannot test without real PostgreSQL database.
// Cannot test without sending real SMS.
```

### What does loose coupling look like?

```java
public class TradeService {
    private final NotificationService notifier;   // interface
    private final TradeRepository tradeRepo;       // interface

    public TradeService(NotificationService notifier, TradeRepository tradeRepo) {
        this.notifier = notifier;
        this.tradeRepo = tradeRepo;
    }

    public void executeTrade(Trade trade) {
        tradeRepo.save(trade);                     // could be Postgres, MongoDB, InMemory
        notifier.send(trade.getAccountId(), "Trade executed"); // could be SMS, Email, Mock
    }
}
// Swap SMS to Email: inject EmailService. Zero changes to TradeService.
// Test: inject MockNotificationService + InMemoryTradeRepository. No real DB or SMS.
```

### What are the four signs of tight coupling?

```java
// Sign 1: new inside the class
private final SmsService sms = new SmsService(); // tight
private final NotificationService notifier;       // loose (injected)

// Sign 2: concrete class in method signature
public void process(PostgresTradeRepository repo) { } // tight
public void process(TradeRepository repo) { }         // loose (interface)

// Sign 3: static method calls to concrete utilities
String hash = CryptoUtils.sha256(data);  // tight
String hash = hashService.hash(data);    // loose (inject interface)

// Sign 4: direct field access on another class
account.balance = amount;    // tight (breaks encapsulation)
account.deposit(amount);     // loose (uses public method)
```

### What are the three benefits of loose coupling?

```java
// Benefit 1: Swappable at runtime
// Production: new TradeService(new SmsService(), new PostgresRepo())
// A/B test:   new TradeService(new EmailService(), new PostgresRepo())
// Migration:  new TradeService(new SmsService(), new MongoRepo())

// Benefit 2: Testable with mocks
@org.junit.jupiter.api.Test
void executeTrade_sendsNotification() {
    NotificationService mock = org.mockito.Mockito.mock(NotificationService.class);
    TradeRepository mockRepo = org.mockito.Mockito.mock(TradeRepository.class);
    TradeService service = new TradeService(mock, mockRepo);
    service.executeTrade(new Trade("TRD-001", "RELIANCE", amount));
    org.mockito.Mockito.verify(mock).send("SAV2001", "Trade executed");
    // Test runs in 10ms. No database. No SMS gateway.
}

// Benefit 3: Maintainable
// SmsService switches from Twilio to AWS SNS: zero changes to TradeService.
```

### How do tight and loose coupling compare?

| Aspect | Tight Coupling | Loose Coupling |
|--------|---------------|----------------|
| Dependency | Concrete class | Interface |
| Creation | new inside class | Injected from outside |
| Swappability | Requires code change | Zero code change |
| Testability | Needs real dependencies | Inject mocks |
| Change propagation | Breaks consumers | Isolated |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- In Spring, every @Service with constructor injection of interfaces is loose coupling in action.
- The Dependency Inversion Principle (D in SOLID) formalizes loose coupling: depend on abstractions, not on concrete classes.
- Loose coupling enables microservice architecture: services communicate through interfaces (REST APIs, message queues), not through direct class dependencies.
